### Replication of the Problem

```js
const listr = new Listr(
  [
    {
      task() {
        throw Error("Everything is broken");
      },
    },
  ],
  { exitOnError: false },
);
await listr.run({});

if (listr.errors.length > 0) console.log("Fix me");
else console.log("No errors, release to production!");
```

Expected: “Fix me”
Actual: “No errors, release to production!”

### Problem

If exitOnError: false is set, then all the errors are ignored, and the `tasks.errors` is an empty array. The latter is unexpected.

I understand this is the designed behavior ([relevant comment](https://github.com/listr2/listr2/issues/645#issuecomment-1816160663)). It can be fixed using `collectErrors: "minimal"` ([documentation](https://listr2.kilic.dev/task/error-handling.html#collected-errors)).

But the default seems to be very dangerous. If `listr.errors` is defined ([documentation](https://listr2.kilic.dev/api/listr2/classes/Listr.html#errors)), and is an empty array, then the empty array really means that there were no errors. With the default behavior it is straight up wrong. The main failure case is consumers’ `if (errors.length)` checks not working.

The safest solution could be to change the default from `false` to `"minimal"`. The performance concerns that led to disabling it by default seems to apply mostly to the `"full"` mode.

Alternatively, `errors` can be set to undefined unless `collectedErrors` is set. Then there is no misleading consumers.


